{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, new user passwords must be at least 12 characters long.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed pwquality configuration sets minlen = 12, both PAM password stacks reference pam_pwquality, an 11-character candidate was rejected specifically for being shorter than 12 characters, and a compliant 16-character candidate was accepted. The same configuration and behavior remained after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e3284793e010415a","cmd-6d42807d4a674480","cmd-b13c99c3ae1c4364","cmd-8f53d4190db54b1e"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"On node1, new user passwords must use at least three different character classes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed pwquality configuration sets minclass = 3, both PAM password stacks reference pam_pwquality, a 16-character candidate containing only two classes was rejected specifically for having fewer than three classes, and a four-class candidate was accepted. Enforcement persisted after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e3284793e010415a","cmd-6d42807d4a674480","cmd-b13c99c3ae1c4364","cmd-8f53d4190db54b1e"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating the pwquality policy file was the required implementation (cmd-e3284793e010415a), and scheduling the reboot was required by the provider-wide restart evidence protocol (cmd-a5205c557e7a4f71). The remaining commands were discovery or bounded verification. Afterward, node1 had no failed services, unchanged network exposure apart from process IDs, and healthy package-manager state; temporary-file differences were normal systemd runtime directories produced by the reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e3284793e010415a","cmd-a5205c557e7a4f71","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both password-policy outcomes are satisfied. The restart protocol is coherent: node1 was running before restart with boot ID 9bdcc129-3d25-48ff-ad64-56be332fb91e, acknowledged a scheduled reboot, briefly reset the connection, returned running with boot ID 4a81e8f9-24aa-4a14-97c8-4edb510dc9c9, and retained the policy and enforcement behavior. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the identity change.","limitations":["Behavior was tested through pwscore with PAM integration verified from configuration, rather than through an end-to-end password change using passwd. This is strong but slightly less direct evidence of the complete user-password workflow.","No separate application data or cluster recovery probe was applicable to this single standalone node; recovery evidence covers system state, root filesystem availability, PAM configuration, password-policy behavior, and baseline listening services."]}
